---
import ResearchFigure from "../../components/ResearchFigure.astro";
import type { CasePart } from "../types";
import procedure from "./emotion-figure-1-original.jpeg";
import coverage from "./emotion-measurement-coverage-v2.png";
import behavior from "./emotion-figure-3-original.jpeg";
import activation from "./emotion-figure-4-original.png";
import decoding from "./emotion-figure-5-original.jpeg";
import functionalDecoding from "./emotion-figure-6-original.jpeg";
import theory from "./emotion-figure-7-original.jpeg";

interface Props {
  part: CasePart;
}
const { part } = Astro.props;
const article = "https://doi.org/10.1037/emo0001683";
---

{part === "intro" && (
  <p>
    I contributed across the behavioral and fNIRS analyses, including
    preprocessing, activation analysis, interbrain synchrony and multivariate
    classification.
  </p>
)}

{part === "visual" && (
  <section
    class="portfolio-section emotion-task-section"
    aria-labelledby="emotion-task-heading"
  >
    <h2 id="emotion-task-heading">The paired task</h2>
    <ResearchFigure
      src={procedure}
      eager
      class="emotion-procedure"
      alt="Original Figure 1. A and B show the paired fNIRS setup and masked participant photograph. C shows initial and final resting periods around one neutral and four negative-image blocks. For both regulator and target, a negative image and ratings precede an eight-second interaction, followed by the same image and new ratings. Separate rows preserve the cognitive-reappraisal and arithmetic-distraction procedures and all phase timings."
    >
      <a href={article}>Figure 1, Yang et al., 2026.</a> Thirty-four pairs of
      women took part. In panel C, CR denotes reinterpreting the image
      (cognitive reappraisal); DIS denotes mental arithmetic (distraction). The
      regulator offered the strategy and the target responded; both rated the
      same negative image before and after the interaction.
    </ResearchFigure>
  </section>
)}

{part === "body" && (
  <>
    <nav class="emotion-result-nav" aria-label="Results on this page">
      <a href="#emotion-behavior-heading">Behavior</a>
      <a href="#emotion-activation-heading">Activation</a>
      <a href="#emotion-decoding-heading">Interbrain synchrony</a>
      <a href="#emotion-interpretation-heading">Interpretation</a>
    </nav>
    <section
      class="portfolio-section"
      aria-labelledby="emotion-measurement-heading"
    >
      <h2 id="emotion-measurement-heading">Where we measured</h2>
      <ResearchFigure
        src={coverage}
        class="emotion-coverage"
        alt="Original Figure 2. Frontal and lateral brain views show all sources, detectors and channels across ten regions, including prefrontal, primary motor, primary sensory, Broca and Wernicke regions. The complete color legend identifies the sampled regions."
      >
        <a href={article}>Figure 2.</a> Simultaneous fNIRS recordings yielded
        blood-oxygenation time courses from ten cortical regions per person.
      </ResearchFigure>
    </section>

    <section
      class="portfolio-section"
      aria-labelledby="emotion-behavior-heading"
    >
      <h2 id="emotion-behavior-heading">
        Valence improved, especially after reappraisal
      </h2>
      <p class="portfolio-copy">
        Valence ratings were higher after regulation and higher overall for
        regulators than targets. Reappraisal produced higher postregulation
        valence than distraction; the strategies did not differ reliably before
        regulation. The time-by-strategy interaction was significant, while the
        other valence interactions and all arousal effects were nonsignificant.
      </p>
      <ResearchFigure
        src={behavior}
        class="emotion-behavior"
        alt="Original Figure 3. Four valence distributions show reappraisal and distraction before and after regulation. Individual points, box plots and significance brackets are intact. Postregulation reappraisal has higher ratings than postregulation distraction."
      >
        <a href={article}>Figure 3.</a> Pre-IER and post-IER denote ratings
        before and after interpersonal emotion regulation. Higher ratings
        indicate more positive valence. *** p &lt; .001.
      </ResearchFigure>
    </section>

    <section
      class="portfolio-section"
      aria-labelledby="emotion-activation-heading"
    >
      <h2 id="emotion-activation-heading">
        Activation depended on role and strategy
      </h2>
      <p class="portfolio-copy">
        Role-by-strategy interactions appeared in the frontal pole (FP), left
        and right orbitofrontal cortex (lOFC, rOFC), and right Broca’s area
        (rBroca). Targets showed higher activation during distraction than
        reappraisal in all four regions. During reappraisal, regulators showed
        higher activation than targets in all four; during distraction, targets
        exceeded regulators in rOFC and rBroca.
      </p>
      <ResearchFigure
        src={activation}
        class="emotion-activation"
        alt="Complete original Figure 4, including A frontal pole, B left orbitofrontal cortex, C right orbitofrontal cortex and D right Broca's area. Each panel retains the four role-by-strategy distributions, individual observations, beta-value axes and all reported significance brackets; the shared condition legend remains between the rows."
      >
        <a href={article}>Figure 4A–D.</a> Task-related oxygenated-hemoglobin
        activation estimates. * p &lt; .05; ** p &lt; .01; *** p &lt; .001.
      </ResearchFigure>
    </section>

    <section
      class="portfolio-section"
      aria-labelledby="emotion-decoding-heading"
    >
      <h2 id="emotion-decoding-heading">
        Interbrain patterns distinguished strategy and success
      </h2>
      <p class="portfolio-copy">
        Correlations between the two participants’ regional time courses formed
        a 10 × 10 interbrain-synchronization matrix for each trial. In five-fold
        within-participant cross-validation, classifiers distinguished the
        strategy, regulator success and target success above 50% chance (all
        FDR-corrected p &lt; .001). Success meant a more positive valence rating
        on the second viewing.
      </p>
      <p class="portfolio-copy">
        Feature-importance rankings differed across region pairs for all three
        classifiers. Connections involving the regulator’s frontal pole appeared
        repeatedly among the most informative features.
      </p>
      <ResearchFigure
        src={decoding}
        class="emotion-decoding"
        alt="Complete original Figure 5. A shows strategy, regulator-success and target-success decoding accuracies with all points, distributions, box plots and the 0.50 chance line. B shows the ten highest-ranked interbrain region pairs for each classifier. Lower average ranks indicate greater importance; each pair names the regulator's region first and the target's region second."
      >
        <a href={article}>Figure 5A–B.</a> A: all three decoding tests exceeded
        chance. B: lower average rank indicates greater feature importance;
        pairs are regulator ROI × target ROI. These tests evaluated held-out
        trials within participants, rather than generalization to new
        participants.
      </ResearchFigure>
    </section>

    <section
      class="portfolio-section"
      aria-labelledby="emotion-interpretation-heading"
    >
      <h2 id="emotion-interpretation-heading">
        Exploratory functional interpretation
      </h2>
      <p class="portfolio-copy">
        Neurosynth analyses linked the four regions to terms from the
        neuroimaging literature. These associations informed an interpretation
        in which regulators support monitoring and guidance, while targets
        engage different demands when accepting a reinterpretation or doing
        arithmetic. This functional account remains exploratory.
      </p>
      <ResearchFigure
        src={functionalDecoding}
        class="emotion-functional-decoding"
        alt="Complete original Figure 6. Four panels retain the brain slices, coordinates and associated term clouds for frontal pole, left and right orbitofrontal cortex and right Broca's area. These are literature-based Neurosynth functional associations, not activation maps measured in this study's participants."
      >
        <a href={article}>Figure 6A–D.</a> Literature-based functional decoding:
        brain locations and associated cognitive terms for each region.
      </ResearchFigure>
      <details class="portfolio-methods">
        <summary>The paper’s proposed theoretical model</summary>
        <ResearchFigure
          src={theory}
          class="emotion-theory"
          alt="Original Figure 7, the authors' proposed model. A regulator monitoring-and-guidance system is paired with a target inhibitory-and-executive system, with interbrain synchrony between them and valence outcomes for both roles. Directional arrows express the theoretical interpretation."
        >
          <a href={article}>Figure 7.</a> The authors’ proposed integration of
          behavioral, activation and synchrony findings. Its arrows depict a
          candidate mechanism; the symmetric correlations do not establish
          causal transmission between brains.
        </ResearchFigure>
      </details>
    </section>

    <details class="portfolio-methods emotion-statistics">
      <summary>Reported statistical comparisons</summary>
      <h3>Behavior</h3>
      <p>
        Valence main effects: role, F(1, 231) = 69.08, η² = .23; time, F =
        70.91, η² = .23; strategy, F = 36.68, η² = .14. The time × strategy
        interaction was F = 22.40, η² = .09 (all p &lt; .001). Other valence
        interactions had Fs &lt; 3.43, ps &gt; .065. Arousal had no significant
        main effects or interactions (Fs &lt; 3.63, ps &gt; .058).
      </p>
      <p>
        Regulators versus targets: t(231) = 8.31, d = 1.01; post- versus
        preregulation: t = 8.42, d = 1.02; reappraisal versus distraction after
        regulation: t = 7.63, d = 1.31 (all p &lt; .001). Before regulation, the
        strategy comparison was t = .94, p = .350, d = .16.
      </p>
      <h3>Activation</h3>
      <p>
        Role × strategy interactions, all with F(1, 99): FP, 17.20, p &lt; .001,
        η² = .15; lOFC, 9.30, p = .003, η² = .09; rOFC, 26.09, p &lt; .001, η² =
        .21; rBroca, 10.95, p = .001, η² = .10. Strategy main effects were also
        reported in FP (F = 6.31, p = .014, η² = .06) and rOFC (F = 9.45, p =
        .003, η² = .09).
      </p>
      <ul class="emotion-comparisons">
        <li>
          <strong>FP:</strong> targets DIS &gt; CR, t(99) = 4.70, p &lt; .001, d
          = 1.14; regulators &gt; targets under CR, t = 4.02, p &lt; .001, d =
          .98.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>lOFC:</strong> targets DIS &gt; CR, t(99) = 2.35, p = .021, d
          = .57; regulators &gt; targets under CR, t = 3.02, p = .002, d = .78.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>rOFC:</strong> targets DIS &gt; CR, t(99) = 5.79, p &lt; .001,
          d = 1.40; targets &gt; regulators under DIS, t = 2.86, p = .005, d =
          .69; targets &lt; regulators under CR, t = −4.37, p &lt; .001, d =
          −1.06.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>rBroca:</strong> targets DIS &gt; CR, t(99) = 3.19, p = .002,
          d = .77; targets &gt; regulators under DIS, t = 2.54, p = .013, d =
          .61; targets &lt; regulators under CR, t = −2.14, p = .034, d = −.52.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Synchrony-based decoding</h3>
      <p>
        One-sample tests against .50 chance, all with t(33): strategy, 3.78, d =
        1.31; regulator success, 7.41, d = 2.58; target success, 4.73, d = 1.65
        (all FDR-corrected p &lt; .001). Importance rankings differed across
        region pairs for strategy, regulator success and target success: χ²(99)
        = 592.68, 754.58 and 785.33, respectively (all p &lt; .001).
      </p>
    </details>

    <details class="portfolio-methods">
      <summary>Analysis methods and study scope</summary>
      <p>
        After quality checks and preprocessing, activation analyses used
        task-related oxygenated-hemoglobin estimates. Behavioral and activation
        models included a dyad random intercept. Trial-level Pearson
        correlations between ten regions per person supplied the interbrain
        features. Support-vector machines used five-fold within-participant
        cross-validation, with oversampling to balance classes when needed;
        permutation importance ranked the features.
      </p>
      <p>
        The study recruited 68 female university students in 34 dyads. Its
        sampling and cortical coverage limit generalization, and the
        meta-analytic functional account remains a candidate explanation for
        further hypothesis-driven research.
      </p>
    </details>
  </>
)}

<style>
  .emotion-result-nav {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.6rem 1.4rem;
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
    padding-block: 1rem;
    border-block: 1px solid var(--portfolio-line);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
  .emotion-result-nav a {
    color: var(--portfolio-accent);
    text-underline-offset: 0.2em;
  }
  h2[id] {
    scroll-margin-top: 6rem;
  }
  .emotion-task-section {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
  :global(.emotion-procedure),
  :global(.emotion-activation),
  :global(.emotion-decoding),
  :global(.emotion-functional-decoding),
  :global(.emotion-theory) {
    max-width: 68rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
  }
  :global(.emotion-coverage) {
    max-width: 48rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
  }
  :global(.emotion-behavior) {
    max-width: 46rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
  }
  .emotion-statistics h3 {
    margin: 1.6rem 0 0.5rem;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    color: var(--portfolio-ink);
  }
  .emotion-comparisons {
    padding-left: 1.3em;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }
  .emotion-comparisons li + li {
    margin-top: 0.8rem;
  }
</style>
